The '''Family rule system''' was a informal system of governance that emerged in pockets of the [[Kingdom of Burawa]] after the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa]]. The system resembled an early version of {{w|Feudalism}} where warrior nobility and their families were given ownership over critical defense points in the kingdom to better protect from outside invasions. 

== History ==
The king [[Iacob II]] had provided his elite soldiers from the war pieces of defensible land in it's new territories in the [[Buran River Valley]] and the [[Iracwi Highlands]] for them to rule under his oversight.

The family rule system was never formalized and was seen as a reward for those who fought in the conquest, it was expected that the lands given to the warriors were to be returned to the kingdom after a period of time, but instead the lands were continued to be passed down to the successors of those warriors due to constant incursions of invaders from neighboring lands prompting a need for more localized administration.

The system had been formalized into a title scheme by [[Marcel II]] as the [[Hoggaanka system]] by decree in 1105 to recognize and award more territory and recognize the powers of these families and their relationship with the King.